<title>LEBANON: Lebanon's Mediterranee bank profits up.</title>
<headline>Lebanon's Mediterranee bank profits up.</headline>
<dateline>BEIRUT 1997-06-27</dateline>
<text>
<p>Lebanon's Banque de la Mediterranee reported a 38.9 percent increase in net profits in 1996 and a 92.63 percent rise in customer deposits, according to results published in Beirut newspapers on Friday.</p>
<p>Assets climbed 40.3 percent while credits and loans to customers rose by the same percentage.</p>
<p>It issued the following results (in billions of Lebanese pounds. Rankings according to Bankdata Financial services):</p>
<p>			  1996  (ranking)     1995  (ranking)</p>
<p>Net profits	   41.94  2		  30.20   2</p>
<p>Assets	     4,335.00  2	     3,090.96   1</p>
<p>Customer deposits  248.56  2		 129.03   2</p>
<p>Loans		1,735.85  1	     1,311.00   1</p>
<p>Banque de la Mediterranee is 100 percent owned by Prime Minister Rafik al-Hariri. According to the almanac of the Lebanese Banks Association it has 24 branches and 531 employees.</p>
<p>($1= 1552 Lebanese pounds)</p>
<p>-- Beirut editorial  (9611) 602057 745308 745309</p>
